@page "/docs/cli/templates"

@* izzy new starter|kit <id>: starters and kits, the name and folder, options, --dry-run, and the one
   starter that dotnet new has too (Cli.cs New, TemplateExport.ShortName; Templates/Program.cs exports
   only what needs no account). *@

<ArticlePage Section="cli" Slug="templates">
    <Lead>
        A template is a whole app to start from. <Code>izzy new</Code> creates the app in a new folder, with the name
        and options you give it. To browse the templates, see <Anchor Href="templates">Templates</Anchor>, where each
        one has a picture and its options.
    </Lead>
    <ChildContent>
        <Heading Level="2">Starters and kits</Heading>
        <List Gap="2">
            <ListItem>
                <strong>A starter</strong> is an app's shell: the layout, the theme, a home page, and account pages when
                you ask for them. Use one when you'll build the pages yourself.
            </ListItem>
            <ListItem>
                <strong>A kit</strong> is a working app built on a starter, with its pages, data and sample records. Use
                one when your app is close to it, such as a CRM.
            </ListItem>
        </List>

        <Heading Level="2">Create an app</Heading>
        <TextBlock>Run <Code>izzy new</Code> with the kind, the template's name, and a name for your app:</TextBlock>
        <Code Block Language="bash">@Starter</Code>
        <Code Block Language="bash">@Kit</Code>
        <TextBlock>
            izzy creates a folder named after the app. To put the app somewhere else, add <Code>-o</Code> with the folder.
            Then run the app:
        </TextBlock>
        <Code Block Language="bash">@Run</Code>
        <TextBlock>
            Every app has a <Code>README.md</Code> that says how to run it, where things are and how to add to it. An app
            with accounts says how to log in: Izzy CRM's lists the sample team it makes and their password.
        </TextBlock>
        <TextBlock>
            To list the templates, run <Code>izzy list starter</Code> or <Code>izzy list kit</Code>.
        </TextBlock>

        <Heading Level="2">Options</Heading>
        <TextBlock>
            Each template has options for its render modes, accounts and theme. Add them to the command:
        </TextBlock>
        <Code Block Language="bash">izzy new starter topnav -n Admin --auth Individual --preset Compact --brand teal</Code>
        <TextBlock>
            To list a template's options, run <Code>izzy describe</Code> with its kind and id, such as
            <Code>izzy describe starter topnav</Code>. A kit decides some of its starter's options for you, so it lists fewer.
            The starters' options are explained in <Anchor Href="docs/get-started/create-app">Create an app</Anchor>.
        </TextBlock>

        <Heading Level="2">See the files first</Heading>
        <TextBlock>
            To list the files a template would create without creating them, add <Code>--dry-run</Code>. For the same
            list as data, add <Code>--json</Code>.
        </TextBlock>
        <TextBlock>
            If the folder already has a file with different content, izzy stops and writes nothing. To compare
            the two, add <Code>--diff</Code>. To replace the file, add <Code>--overwrite</Code>.
        </TextBlock>

        <Heading Level="2">With dotnet new</Heading>
        <TextBlock>
            The IzzyBlazor App starter is also a <Code>dotnet new</Code> template, with the same options:
        </TextBlock>
        <Code Block Language="bash">@DotnetNew</Code>
        <TextBlock>
            To install the templates package, see <Anchor Href="docs/get-started/create-app">Create an app</Anchor>.
            The other starters and the kits are free with an IzzyBlazor account, and come from <Code>izzy new</Code>
            after <Anchor Href="docs/cli/account">izzy login</Anchor>.
            Their code is yours to use under the <Anchor Href="docs/cli/license">template license</Anchor>.
        </TextBlock>

        <Heading Level="2">Next steps</Heading>
        <List Gap="2">
            <ListItem><Anchor Href="docs/cli/blocks">Blocks</Anchor>: add a hero, pricing or a sign-up form to a page.</ListItem>
            <ListItem><Anchor Href="docs/cli/crud">CRUD pages</Anchor>: create a page to display, add, edit and delete a model's records.</ListItem>
        </List>
    </ChildContent>
</ArticlePage>

@code {
    private const string Starter = "izzy new starter sidebar -n MyApp";
    private const string Kit = "izzy new kit crm -n MyApp";
    private const string Run = "cd MyApp\ndotnet run";
    private const string DotnetNew = "dotnet new starter-blazor -n MyApp";
}
